1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.635
**Host (likely Eric):** All right, welcome to Biblical Manhood. We're going to start as the rest of you all find your seats. It's our joy to have you here. This is going to be an awesome journey. If you've been here before or if this is your first time, we look forward to what God is going to do through this in your lives and in your family's lives and in our community's lives. And so, let's open in prayer, then I'm going to introduce Tim and we will get started. So, let's pray.

2
00:00:37.635 --> 00:01:09.264
Heavenly Father, thank you so much for this beautiful day. Thank you for this opportunity to engage with you about what it means to be a man. And Father, I pray that you would speak to each one of us at the exact place we're at in our lives and you would help us to know the next step we're supposed to take. I pray for Tim as he ministers to us through your word. In Jesus' name, amen.

3
00:01:09.264 --> 00:02:14.924
**Tim Hawks:** Well, good morning gentlemen. Don't hesitate to find a place where you can see. So if there's a pole in between you, pick up your chair and move it. We are men. We don't have to be orderly. We can sit where we want to sit. There's nobody in here to tell us to keep the row straight or stay where you're supposed to be. I'm excited to have you guys here. I'm proud of you for being here. I think that coming together as a group of men to begin to discuss identity issues is something that's relatively unheard of in today's society. And for a group of guys to get up early in the morning and to get here, especially since I see a number of high school students that are here that are probably going to hit the weight room as soon as this is over, we're proud of you guys and we're excited for you to be here.

4
00:02:14.924 --> 00:02:44.151
This course, Biblical Manhood, we started teaching it about 20 years ago. It's benefited people in tremendous ways. You don't just hear it once and then it's over with. It's something to be reminded about again and again and again because we're talking about identity. Who are we as men? What does that look like? How do we move forward in our masculinity in a way that creates something positive for the world?

5
00:02:44.151 --> 00:03:09.374
In order for us to get started today, I think it's only fair that I give you all my presuppositions, the presuppositions that underlie this study, to lay the foundation. Then we're going to spend some time having you guys get to know each other in your group. Let's dive in. You have an outline in front of you, introduction to biblical manhood.

6
00:03:09.374 --> 00:03:57.818
Here's my presuppositions. Number one: manhood is in a state of confusion. Since World War II, we have experienced what I would call the feminization of our culture. Here's my presupposition: when God created male and female, he gave them different operating systems. For those of you who live in the world of computers, you get what I'm talking about. There's a different OS. Men have a different mode of feelings, of thinking, of how they attack the world, of how they are oriented in the world. They approach it differently than women. That's called masculinity. And it just so happens that the software, the wiring of men, fits with the hardware, the physiology of men. And those two are complementary.

7
00:03:57.818 --> 00:04:35.853
Now, if you're coming today and you say, "Well, you don't believe in God. I don't believe in a biblical worldview." Just look at nature. If you're looking from a purely naturalistic standpoint, the wiring, the feelings, the abilities, the mode, the approach to life and the physiology of a man, they're integrated. They work together. I believe the same thing is true of women: women have a different mode of feelings, of thinking, of how to approach the world, and their physiology, their hardware, matches that. Men and women are complementary and they're different.

8
00:04:35.853 --> 00:05:16.290
So what happens in a society where one of those operating systems is given incredible value and one of those is said to be subhuman? Some of you will remember the days when the question was being raised on a regular basis: "Men, have you gotten in touch with your feminine side?" Implication: men start all the wars, men do all the cheating, men ruin the world, and therefore if women were running the world, or if men were more like women, then the world would be a better place. So you need to get in touch with your feminine side.

9
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:36.309
Here's the confusion. If you're a man, you don't have a feminine side, because you're a man. Therefore, what do you get in touch with? What you tend to get in touch with is not male or female. It's something weird. And therefore men live in this state of confusion.

10
00:05:36.309 --> 00:06:48.775
There are a number of reasons why this has come about. The first cause is society. Quickest history lesson in the world: the definition of manhood passed on from father to son throughout the history of the world. Many civilizations actually developed rites of passage where definitions were explained and laid out. The Jewish culture, the bar mitzvah, where a boy is told, okay, this is what a boy's like. You're no longer one of those. Now you're a man. You join the community of men. Lots and lots of tribes and cultures around the world have a rite of passage. By the way, in America, we have no rite of passage. There's never a time when all the boys collectively realize we are expected from this point forward to act differently, and then a definition is given as to what it means to be masculine. That's missing in our society. Part of what we're going to be doing in our time together is building a plan to help boys understand when that transition takes place and what that looks like.

11
00:06:48.775 --> 00:07:52.433
But throughout the history of the world, fathers and sons worked side by side. A boy developed an understanding of what it meant to be a man by living in the shadow of his father's sweat and his father's masculine ideas. That was normal until the industrial revolution. In the late 1800s, people left the agrarian lifestyle and they went to work in the factory. Factory work was hard work. Factory work was long hours. And dad found himself separated from his boys for many hours in the day. In the 1800s, it was often 12 hours a day, 6 days a week, before the unions came in and kind of helped organize a little bit better work style or life. So people transitioned from a boy having dad working with them, teaching them day by day, side by side, to living in a world where dad was gone all the time. That took place during the industrial revolution.

12
00:07:52.433 --> 00:08:31.269
During that period of time, men moved from being raised primarily in the company of their fathers to being raised primarily in the company of women. How many of you guys went to school where the majority of the elementary teachers were women? You're being socialized primarily by women. When I say that, I'm not making a negative value judgment. But if father is gone and son doesn't spend much time with dad and lives in a world that's primarily constructed by the feminine, what happens to your understanding of who you are as a man? Society.

13
00:08:31.269 --> 00:09:21.715
Second major trend in society was a spiritual revolution. Up until the mid to late 1800s, the world believed that there was an outside source of truth. Revelation came from God and there was an absolute understanding of how the world was supposed to work. If you could get in touch with that absolute, then you knew how to navigate life. As the Western world in particular began to move away from a belief that there was a God, then definitions became completely based on personal opinion. If there is a way that men have been wired, we certainly stopped looking to some kind of an absolute definition coming from revealed truth and began to have a conversation within society about who are we really anyway.

14
00:09:21.715 --> 00:10:09.759
That conversation took a very different turn in the 1960s and the 1970s in the US when we see a major social revolution taking place. As the feminist movement began to redefine roles within society, there began to become a collective discussion as to whether gender actually was real or whether we made it up. In the 60s and 70s, conversations were taking place like this: well, men and women are really not different at all. The only difference is the way they're raised. And if we would quit giving girls Barbies and quit giving boys GI Joes, then what we would find is that boys and girls would just kind of think and be and act the same way.

15
00:10:09.759 --> 00:10:40.588
Gentlemen, we pride ourselves in the Western world of being scientific, don't we? So if you're just purely going on the basis of biology, that idea is stupid. Think about it. That's stupid. You can stand a naked man and a naked woman in front of you and if you looked at them, you would see that they are different, right? But we as a society are having an experiment where we're saying they're all the same.

16
00:10:40.588 --> 00:11:18.623
In the 80s and 90s, they started doing brain research on men and women and came out with overwhelming evidence that the way men's brains are wired and the way women's brains are wired are significantly different and therefore they think different, which you would think would settle the discussion. But here we are in 2015 taking boys and girls out of Target department store because again as a society we're struggling with the idea of whether there really is a boy or really is a girl or whether there's no real gender at all.

17
00:11:18.623 --> 00:12:11.871
So the challenge before us, gentlemen, is do we live in a world of reality or do we live in a world of social constructs where whoever happens to be politically correct or politically in power or who has the media or the muscle within industry gets to decide reality. It's kind of strange for Western society that says that we believe in science, we believe in reality, we believe in a physical world, to come up with things that are so strange. But that's actually what's happening. And I think it's going to be more severe going into the future. People are going to have a difficult time figuring out who they are because you're going to be told you're not. There is not a gender. So it starts out with society.

18
00:12:11.871 --> 00:13:33.947
The second cause is family dynamics. What shapes this lack of understanding of what it means to be a man? A lot of it has to do with family dynamics. Some of you grew up in patriarchy where dad ruled. Some of you grew up in a family of matriarchy where dad pretended like he ruled but everybody knew that whatever mom said is what happened in our family. Some of you grew up in cases where the two people that you loved the most in life, that you looked to for your security, your identity, your happiness, the two people that you thought loved you so much that they would always be there for you, decided that they weren't going to do that and they got divorced and dad moved away. And you go, what does that mean? What does that say? How do I deal with that? Some of you grew up in abusive situations where the people that were supposed to love you, care for you, teach you, explain life to you, actually mistreated you or took advantage of you. Family dynamics play a huge role in whether or not you get this definition of what it means to be a man.

19
00:13:33.947 --> 00:14:22.792
The third cause is dad himself. I'm talking about your own father. Newsweek ran an article a number of years ago and the title of the article was "Dad is Destiny." It documented numerous years of research pointing to the fact that the biological father of a child plays the biggest role, over any other thing, in their social, their spiritual, their intellectual advantage or benefit in life, and that the biological father is indispensable if you want a child to get the best possible approach to life. And yet 40% of the boys in the US are being raised in the absence of their biological father. What does that do to the confusion that comes in when dad is not present?

20
00:14:22.792 --> 00:14:48.015
The article went on to point out that this is true of both boys and girls. Girls learn as much about femininity from playing off the masculinity of their biological father as boys do embracing it. Depending on your relationship with your dad, that could play a major factor in your understanding or lack of understanding of what it means to be masculine.

21
00:14:48.015 --> 00:16:01.682
Let me just read you a clip from the article called "The Trouble with Boys," Newsweek magazine. "One of the most reliable predictors of whether a boy will succeed or fail in high school rests on a single question. Does he have a man in his life to look up to? Too often the answer is no. The high rates of divorce and single motherhood have created a generation of fatherless boys. In every kind of neighborhood, rich or poor, an increasing number of boys, now a startling 40%, are being raised without their biological dads. Psychologists say that grandfathers and uncles can help" (and we'll talk about the role of men and mentors in your life) "but emphasize that an adolescent boy without a father figure is like an explorer without a map." Think about that. An adolescent boy without a father figure is like an explorer without a map, wandering through life trying to make it up on their own as they go along. Some of you would say, that was me. That was me. And you know exactly how that feels.

22
00:16:01.682 --> 00:16:32.110
And that is especially true for poor boys and boys who are struggling in school. Older males model self-restraint and solid work habits for younger ones. And whether they're breathing down their necks about grades or admonishing them to show up for school on time, an older man reminds a boy in a million different ways that school is critical to their life mission. It goes on and on and talks about the benefits of dad.

23
00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:42.119
So manhood is in a state of confusion. Some of it society, some of it family dynamics, some of it our relationship with our father.

24
00:16:42.119 --> 00:17:12.147
My second presupposition is that confused men create major problems. Garrison Keillor once said, "These are not good times for guys. It seems like manhood is a problem to overcome rather than a status to obtain." Time magazine ran an article called "Are Men Really That Bad?" And the cover of the article was a business suit with a pig's head on it. These are leading publications raising the question, are men really that bad?

25
00:17:12.147 --> 00:17:46.979
Here's something important. Margaret Mead, feminist, secular anthropologist, one of the leading anthropologists in the past century. Here's what she said. She said the central problem of every society is to define appropriate roles for men. Now think about that. It's not: the central problem for every society is to find a clean water source, to develop an agricultural system that works, to find ways to educate the next generation. The single greatest problem in studying the history of humanity is to find appropriate roles for men.

26
00:17:46.979 --> 00:18:20.209
Here's what I believe, and we'll unpack this during the study: men are wired with a feeling of destiny. I have within me the capability of making a difference in the world. And I'm either going to lean into the difficulty and challenge that places before me, which moves me away from my selfishness and my lazy feelings into something positive that makes a difference, or I move backwards into putting my masculine energy into taking care of me. It's all about me.

27
00:18:20.209 --> 00:19:41.084
In a society where men are challenged to own and move forward using their masculine energy to change the world and make things better, and that's the core identity of man, what we see are men rise to the occasion, do incredible things. But in a society where men are basically told, sit back, don't do anything, keep to yourself, and indulge your feminine side, men become a drag. Is that true? Just look at the crime rates in the United States. 81% of the violent crimes in the United States are committed by men. Chances are, much greater than not, when a policeman pulls a drunk driver over on the side of the road, the person behind the wheel is going to be a man. When it comes to violence against women and children, it's going to be a man. When it comes to murder, it's going to be a man. 63% of the property crimes in the United States are committed by men. What's gone wrong there? What's taken place in a culture where the predominance of problems related to public safety and basic civil living are coming from men? The problem is that confused men create major negative issues.

28
00:19:41.084 --> 00:20:11.111
The third one, letter C there: confused men settle for less. I want to introduce you guys to a concept called the conventional model of manhood. The conventional model of manhood says this: that men really only have one significant purpose in life, and that is to earn a living. And as long as I'm earning a living and paying the bills for my family, then I've pretty much done what I need to do.

29
00:20:11.111 --> 00:21:07.963
I remember my father-in-law who was raised during this period of time where this was really a strong impulse, conventional model manhood. I remember when he retired. He's passed away now, but great guy. I love the guy. He retired after building a successful business in Arkansas, made good money, did really well. And when he retired, he said, "Now what am I going to do?" And all his friends said, "Well, what did you like to do before you started working?" And so he thinks back 40 years. Think about it. 40 years. He said, "Well, I used to like to fish." And they said, "Well, then you need to fish." And so he sells the family home, buys a condo on the lake, goes out, buys a brand new fishing boat, completely outfitted, and he's going to be a fisherman.

30
00:21:07.963 --> 00:21:43.596
I'd come to visit. 5:00 in the morning, he's knocking on my door. "Hey, get out of bed. The fish are biting." We get in the boat. We go out across the lake. I say, "Where are they catching them?" "I don't know." "What are they catching them on?" "I don't know." "When was the last time you went fishing?" "Oh, last Christmas when you were here." So you're dragging me out at 5:00 in the morning to do what you love to do. And you don't do it.

31
00:21:43.596 --> 00:22:48.055
Finally, after 10 years of being miserable, living on the lake, not catching a single fish, he moves back home to the neighborhood that the kids grew up in and spent the rest of his days watching daytime television. Do you know what was on daytime television in the 80s? Young and the Restless. Phil Donahue. Some of you guys don't know that name. It was the somewhat masculine version of Oprah. I guess it was the Ellen version of Oprah. But what he had was he had his TV fixed up where his Wall Street stock ticker was running across the bottom of the TV all the time. So he could watch his life's work go up and down and up and down and up and down as he spent the rest of his life, because he didn't know any other way to live. Like, I'm a man. My deal is to work and if I'm not working, I'm worthless.

32
00:22:48.055 --> 00:23:31.295
So from the time a boy is little, we ask the question, what are you going to be when you grow up? When we run into high school age students, where are you going to go to college? College age students, what are you majoring in? When we meet another man, within the first few minutes, we want to find out what they do for a living because we tend to identify men around what they do. Somebody says, "Yeah, well, I'm a doctor." Oh. "I'm a lawyer." Oh. And we kind of size everybody up on the basis of that. That's kind of how we approach life.

33
00:23:31.295 --> 00:24:24.544
What's been interesting is generationally we see some shifts. The builder generation, which is what my dad, my father-in-law were part of, they didn't view play as a high priority. It was mostly work because we're going to make something of ourselves. We're going to build something. Build and no play. The boomer generation came along and they viewed themselves as work and play. Whereas my family, if we were going to take a vacation when I was a kid, we were going to go visit my grandfather and my grandmother and maybe slip into Disney World for a little day excursion. The boomers come along and vacation now is going skiing in Colorado because we can. We're going to take the family and we're going to go have fun. Work and play.

34
00:24:24.544 --> 00:24:46.163
The millennial generation comes along and it's work to play. "Boss, if you think my life revolves around this company and this job, you better wake up. I'm here because you are getting my services. And in return, I need plenty of time off." It's an interesting switch in manhood there. Work to play.

35
00:24:46.163 --> 00:25:35.809
For all of those folks in the room who have hired a millennial, and we got plenty of millennials in the room here, for all of you who've hired a millennial and you're grumpy about that: let me just tell you that when kids are raised, and the boomers did this, when you raise a kid where all of their waking hours are traveling to play, play soccer, play football, play basketball, play baseball, all his life from the time he's little is traveling to play, and while he's traveling he's holding a handheld game in his hands playing it on the road to get to play, then why wouldn't we culturally think that the younger generation would view themselves as people who play?

36
00:25:35.809 --> 00:26:47.475
Now let me just speak to the millennial generation. One of the challenges for you guys is this: there's a concept today called delayed manhood. And it starts with this kind of thinking. Man, four years of college that my parents paid for, that was really hard. And when I graduate, I need a gap year. I need a year where you guys are going to continue to pay so that I can travel Europe or go hang out and ski for the season. I just need time off to go have a good time. And then I need a couple years where you're going to take me in. I'm going to live with you or you're going to keep me on your cell phone plan, pay my car insurance or whatever, so I can kind of find myself. And so we've got guys that are moving into their early 30s that have not actually started on this idea of what does it mean to construct the life of a man. And you're kind of halfway into your life.

37
00:26:47.475 --> 00:27:17.903
That's why I get calls from wives who say, "Tim, I don't know what to do. I go to work all day and I'm paying the bills and my husband is at home playing World of Warcraft 8 to 10 hours a day." And I say, "How old is he?" And they say, "He's 32 and I don't know what to do, but he's been doing this his whole life and he's just addicted to it."

38
00:27:17.903 --> 00:27:46.329
So part of the challenge that we have is the conventional models of manhood. Even generationally, if you're in the builder generation, what was your view of manhood? If you're in the boomer generation, what's your view of manhood? If you're in the millennial generation, what is it that everybody else in your generation kind of calls manhood? In the face of that, we see that confused men settle for less.

39
00:27:46.329 --> 00:28:08.749
Letter D: there is no lofty vision of manhood that is compelling today. What is it that calls a man away from what the culture says that you can get away with to what your masculinity, the opportunity your masculinity gives you, to do something of value, something of significance, something that will satisfy your soul?

40
00:28:08.749 --> 00:28:39.578
I want to take you to a passage of scripture. Grab a Bible and open it to 1 Corinthians chapter 13. During the course of our study, we're going to from time to time look at passages of scripture. This is primarily not a sermon that I'm going to be preaching, but we are going to reference the Bible from time to time. If you don't have a Bible, look on with a person next to you.

41
00:28:39.578 --> 00:29:16.411
Some of you, as soon as you heard 1 Corinthians chapter 13, you thought, oh, isn't that a feminine chapter? Isn't that all about love and stuff like that? There's an interesting verse that's nestled in the middle of this chapter. And by the way, you may want to pick out a certain color pen that you can use to underline verses in your Bible because over the course of time, we're going to underline some masculine verses. In fact, you can create your own masculine study Bible during our time together.

42
00:29:16.411 --> 00:29:56.848
1 Corinthians 13:11, Paul says this: "When I was a child, I talked like a child. I thought like a child. I reasoned like a child. When I became a man, I put childish ways behind me." That's interesting. What Paul is saying there is clear. There is a time and a place to think and act, to reason and to behave like a child. But there's a point in time where every boy becomes a man. And from that point forward, the childish actions of life are put behind us and we embrace a new reality, and that is manhood.

43
00:29:56.848 --> 00:31:21.726
Here's one of the problems. When you ask a man, "What does it mean to be a man to you? What does that look like? Describe it to me." One of two things is going to come out of the mouth of the person you're talking to. Either you're going to hear progress, looking forward to the future, or you're going to hear regress. When you're talking to a man about what are the great things about being a man, what do you hear from the man? Do you hear a regression to childhood, childish activities? "Oh yeah. I remember when we used to stay out and drink all night. We had the best frat parties. I remember when we used to trash things and damage things and disrespect things and treat women a certain way and sleep with girls." Their history's in their past. There's only so many times that you can tell the younger generation your star football exploits on the field. And you just need to know, guys, while you're waxing eloquent about your past, the younger generation is rolling their eyes, right? Can I get an amen? It's embarrassing for men. It's embarrassing to the young guys for men to be talking about their history all the time.

44
00:31:21.726 --> 00:32:39.798
Or are you talking about the future? Are you talking about your goals and things that you accomplish as a man? Like, I look forward to being a man because I look forward to loving a woman. And I know women are the hardest creatures on the planet to figure out, but I'm a man and men figure things out. And so I'm going to spend my life learning how to love this woman because that's what men do. I'm a man. I can do this. And I look forward to fathering a son. And I look forward to building a family name. And I look forward to teaching my girls what it looks like to be treated like a woman and to have a man treat her well. And I look forward to developing a sense of our family's got character. And I look forward to starting and developing a ministry because my life is going to leave an impact on the world. That's what it means to be a man. I've got a vision for my future and I get up every day and I connect what I do today to that vision.

45
00:32:39.798 --> 00:33:05.822
Or I regress and I talk about those were the good old days. That's when it was fun to be a man. Now it's just drudgery and I got responsibility, as if responsibility is a terrible thing and my life is so difficult and people don't understand me. Guys, those are not masculine feelings. We'll talk about where those come from in a couple sessions.

46
00:33:05.822 --> 00:34:09.080
The guy who developed this study, Robert Lewis, talking about a lofty vision of manhood. Robert Lewis was a pastor of Fellowship Bible Church, Little Rock. And 20 years ago, he approached me and he said, "Tim, I've been teaching the study to my men. It's the greatest thing we've ever done." Just had incredible impact on men. And I said, "Can we teach it live?" And he said, "Yeah, I'm telling you, go ahead and teach it." The content of what we're doing here, we've updated it a bit. We've put some of our own stories in, but the content of this is basically Robert Lewis's stuff. So I want to give him full credit for that. We're going to introduce you to a couple of his books along the way that'll be helpful for you, particularly *Raising a Modern-Day Knight*: how do you go about helping a boy grow up and what does that look like.

47
00:34:09.080 --> 00:35:10.336
Robert was doing a seminar one time with a group of men. He had them all in a room and he said, "How many of you guys here have taught your son how to throw a baseball?" And almost every hand in the room went up. And then he said, "How many of you in here have taught your children something related to their education? Tutored them in math, helped them with their reading." Almost every hand went up. And then he asked the question, "How many of you men have taught your sons how to treat a woman?" A few hands went up. And then he asked this: "How many of you have given your son a definition of what it means to be masculine that would inspire them to live for the noble things in life?" Not one hand in the room went up. There's no lofty vision for manhood today.

48
00:35:10.336 --> 00:36:06.788
Clearly, we're much more comfortable teaching our sons academics and athletics than we are some of the most important things in life. Because relating to women and relating to them well is more important than throwing a baseball. And I think everybody agrees with that. I'd rather have my son throw like a girl but treat girls with respect than throw like a boy and treat girls badly. Now, I have daughters, so I have motivation. And hopefully some of you guys have raised godly men as sons, but you'll feel that as your kids get older. We are not comfortable teaching our sons an identity or a vision of who they are as a man because most of us are struggling ourselves to know whether that's the real thing. So no lofty vision of manhood that is compelling to men.

49
00:36:06.788 --> 00:37:18.053
And the last one is this, letter E: the Bible has insight and answers to all of the above. We're going to spend time throughout this study from time to time bouncing into the Bible and looking at verses in the Bible. Now, as we do that, I just want to make it really clear to you guys, and really important. Some of you are geared around the idea that you like to do a Bible study. Can we go verse by verse through a book of the Bible and get this stuff that way? The answer to that question is there are no books in the Bible called the book of manhood. It's just kind of assumed through the Bible as the Bible tells a story that we're going to come across passages. During the course of our study, we're going to be looking at culture. We're going to be looking at family dynamics. We're going to be looking at various things. We're also going to be looking at what does the Bible say about these things.

50
00:37:18.053 --> 00:38:17.308
And for those of you who are here saying, you know, my friend brought me to this. I'm not really into the Bible that much. Is it okay for me to be here? Absolutely. We're glad you're here. What I would ask you to do during this time is kind of put a mindset on to think this way. Okay. I'm going to listen to what's going on here and I'm going to look at what I see in the Bible and I'm going to ask and answer the question: does that make sense? Does what the Bible says correspond to reality? Does that ring true to me? Because I think you're going to find for many of you is you probably haven't heard an explanation of the Bible in a way that really helps, really makes sense, and really see the beauty of how the Bible resonates.

51
00:38:17.308 --> 00:39:08.955
A couple more thoughts as we wrap up our session today. Manhood develops as we address two things that are critically important. The first one is the unfinished business of our past. Men have a tendency to put things off. You're probably not going to run down to the school if your kid gets a bloody nose. You may not even run down to the school if your kid flunks a test. Now, your wife might do that, but you're probably not going to do that. Men have a tendency to put things off because we are crisis-oriented. That's part of our wiring. If he flunks a class, now we're going to have a conversation. We'll deal with this crisis. We're good in crisis. So we deal with crisis.

52
00:39:08.955 --> 00:40:14.215
But oftentimes there are things in our lives: hurts, wounds, frustrations, confusion from the past. And we have a tendency to say, oh, that's old stuff. I'm not going to deal with that. I'm going to put that off. The problem is that oftentimes what we carry around inside of us is like a nuclear waste canister. It's not pain or poison that's going to explode in dramatic fashion like a nuclear bomb. But the waste is there. The frustration is there. The hurt is there. The confusion is there. And over the course of our life, sooner or later that nuclear waste canister begins to leak and grumpiness and frustration and oftentimes laziness, oftentimes addictions, broken relationships begin to come out of the poison of our lives. So part of what we're going to do in this study is really take some time to go back and challenge some issues from the past to be able to understand them and move forward.

53
00:40:14.215 --> 00:41:14.670
Now, as we do this, I want you to understand that some of this for some of you guys is going to be really hard because starting next week, we're going to talk about what you did or did not get from dad. And some of you know that if I don't think about it, I don't get emotional. If I do think about it, it really is really hard for me. And we usually have about 10% of the guys that, can I use a masculine word here, chicken out, that say that's too hard and blame it on well it's early in the morning and so forth. But you just don't want to deal with this and you're never going to get free. The men who find freedom in dealing with some of these issues from their past will testify to how it changed their whole way of life.

54
00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:51.504
Some of you young guys are sitting in the room and you may be here with your dad and you say, boy, this is going to be really awkward. So we'll help you find a group where you guys can discuss issues separately. And so, if you have an issue with that, just stop and talk to one of our guys. We'll kind of break you up. And fathers and sons have the courage to kind of talk through this. So we're going to talk about our unfinished business of the past.

55
00:41:51.504 --> 00:42:51.960
And letter B, we're going to have a clear and compelling personal plan to direct our future. Part of what we're going to do in the second semester is coach you guys at writing your masculinity plan. Many of you have written business plans before. Many of you wrote plans for your education. Many of you guys have put on paper, here's what I need to do to move forward and here's the steps I'm going to take. Well, we're going to actually ask all of you to put on paper, as you move forward in your masculine identity, what steps you need to take. We'll give you a template to do that. We'll coach you to do that. But part of what it means to man up and graduate from this group is to hand me a plan. Okay? So we're going to teach you all how to do that.

56
00:42:51.960 --> 00:43:22.788
Now, some of you all have the wiring of an engineer and your plan will be 30 single-spaced typed pages, and some of you guys you don't, and yours will be scribbled a paragraph on a piece of paper. Don't let that make you nervous. But we're going to help get you to a point where the clarity is so straightforward that you can say, "My next move as a man is" and fill in the blank.

57
00:43:22.788 --> 00:43:33.598
So, looking forward to our time together. It's going to be a great experience. Eric, come on up and share with us what our next steps are.

58
00:43:33.598 --> 00:43:36.000
[Captions end before Eric's next-steps remarks.]
